name wn-lmf
# WN-LMF, the Global WordNet Association's XML for wordnets, read as what it says. A wordnet does not own a word: it
# says things of it. Its identifiers are its keys: how the file points at its entries, senses and synsets; they
# resolve to the things and are recorded nowhere (Semantics: Claims; Storage: Identity).
#   a lexical entry   is the word its Lemma writes
#   a synset          is the composition of the words its members are, in the order it lists them: every wordnet that
#                     lists the same words has the same synset, and their attestations meet there
#   a sense           is the word it is inside, with its synset
#   the lexicon       is named as it names itself (its label)
#   ili               is a type of the highway's interlingual index: read as the concept's content, never as i46360
# What is inside a thing is said of it, every name and value as the wordnet writes it: [word, partOfSpeech, n],
# [synset, Definition, text], [synset, partOfSpeech, n], [synset, lexfile, noun.animal], [synset, ili, concept],
# [word, Sense, sense], [sense, synset, synset]; a relation is its type and what it targets: [synset, hypernym, synset].
grammar xml
# every entry stands on lines of its own: a long file is read in parts, on every core
records
key id
identity Lexicon label
identity LexicalEntry >Lemma.writtenForm
identity Synset members
identity Sense synset within
identity SyntacticBehaviour subcategorizationFrame
refer members Sense within
refer synset Synset
refer SynsetRelation.target Synset
refer SenseRelation.target Sense
refer SyntacticBehaviour.senses Sense
type ili ili
link SenseRelation relType target
link SynsetRelation relType target
